course: dictionary
lesson: 11660
title: Ascribe: saying where something comes from
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: When you ascribe something to a cause or a person, you say where it came from or who made it. It is a formal word, so you will meet it in books, history lessons and exams.
explain: «Mia ascribed her high test score to hard work.» Mia said the reason for her good score was hard work. The word to after ascribe tells you the cause or the person.
explain: «The poem is ascribed to an unknown writer.» Ascribed to means said to come from. People believe the poem came from that writer, but nobody has proved it. Look for the clue words ascribe something to something.
fact: Ascribe means to say that something was caused by something, or that a piece of writing, art or saying was made by a certain person.
fact: «Ascribe her success to hard work» means she succeeded because of hard work.
fact: «Ascribed to» means said to come from, so a poem ascribed to someone may not be proved to be theirs.
fact: Ascribe is a verb: ascribe, ascribes, ascribed, ascribing.
fact: Ascribe is a formal word, so you will see it most in essays, history books and exams.
exercise: «The team ascribed their win to good teamwork.» What did the team say caused their win? | The weather | A late bus | Bad luck | *Good teamwork
exercise: «The old saying is ascribed to a Greek thinker.» What does ascribed to mean here? | Lost in a book | Written in gold ink | *Said to come from | Quickly forgotten
exercise: «Yesterday, Sam ___ his mistake to tiredness.» Which word fits the gap? | *ascribed | ascribes | ascribe | ascribing
test: «Ella ascribes her good spelling to reading every day.» What does Ella say her good spelling comes from? | Being tall | Luck | *Reading every day | A new pencil
test: «The song is ascribed to an unknown writer.» Which is true? | The song is brand new | *Nobody can be sure who wrote it | Everyone knows the writer well | The song was sung by a choir
exam: «The doctor ascribed the illness to a virus, not to bad food.» What does this mean? | Proved to be a joke | *Said to be caused by a virus | Cured by bad food | Written in a virus book
